## t4 - Tests are hermetic by default

The `caller()` helper reads `std::io::stdout().is_terminal()` (line 14), which accesses ambient state—the real terminal. Every test that uses `caller()` touches this ambient state instead of injecting a mock or injecting a hand-written fake environment.

```json
{
  "rule_id": "t4",
  "findings": [
    {
      "rule_id": "t4",
      "verdict": "violation",
      "span": {
        "function": "<file>",
        "line": 14
      },
      "excerpt": "stdout_tty: std::io::stdout().is_terminal(),",
      "reason": "Reads real terminal state instead of injecting a fake or Clock/Environment; all callers touch ambient state.",
      "confidence": "high"
    }
  ]
}
```

## t5 - Guardrail tests sweep, they do not enumerate

Test 4 iterates over `attrs.keys().filter(|key| key.starts_with("kaibo."))`, discovering the population dynamically rather than from a hardcoded list. New kaibo.* attributes would be checked automatically. Test 3 is not a guardrail—it checks a fixed forbidden list, not a property over an open-ended population.

```json
